{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3102","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3102","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3102\",\"parent_revision\":\"16094b246ccd9d3882ef0e7130d1d1c08e09c8cfefc9f74d9b97d50b4f3f18dd\",\"root_revision\":\"16094b246ccd9d3882ef0e7130d1d1c08e09c8cfefc9f74d9b97d50b4f3f18dd\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: Ideas\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis is an exploration/experiment item. The deliverable is an experiment result or design decision with evidence (a prototype on a real plan, a measured comparison, or a written design verdict) \u2014 not a mandate to ship the feature. Origin: comparison of graph-engineering research against the development-harness architecture, grounded against the #3060 agent over-orchestration incident (a task-worker recursively dispatched sub-teams after receiving orchestrator-voiced prose; ~$100 of unplanned spend). Shared design constraint across all seven exploration items in this set: the model keeps the judgment (scoring, declaring, deciding semantic dependencies) but writes it down ONCE, as typed data, at planning time; the mechanical consequence is applied deterministically by machinery that already exists (sam_schema models, DependencyGraph, the implement-feature dispatch loop, work-milestone). No new scheduler, no generic graph runtime \u2014 that larger direction is explicitly out of scope for these items.\\n\\nWhat/why: Convergence rules (\\\"after 3 cycles without progress, report BLOCKED\\\"; grooming loops; recursive follow-up limits) live in skill prose, so each agent counts its own attempts \u2014 or doesn't. Enforcement is inconsistent and invisible: nothing in the data records how many times a task has been attempted, so a task can loop indefinitely across re-dispatches with each new worker starting the count at zero.\\n\\nMitigates: unbounded retry loops across re-dispatches; inconsistent self-counting; inability to observe attempt history after the fact.\\n\\nBenefit lands in: the task claim/state machinery (which already exists and is the natural increment point), incident forensics (attempt counts become queryable), and cost control.\\n\\nProposed exploration: (1) add attempts and max_attempts fields to the SAM task model; (2) increment attempts in the existing claim path; (3) have the claim path refuse a claim past max_attempts, returning an escalation state instead (composes with Item 3's escalate policy); (4) keep prose stating WHY the limit exists; the data enforces WHEN. Trial on a plan containing at least one deliberately failing task.\\n\\nRelated: Item 3 (\\\"Explore declared per-task failure policies (retry/skip/escalate) including executor-loss\\\") \u2014 retry(n) policy reads these counters.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Experiment or design exploration executed as described (prototype on a real plan, measured comparison, or written design verdict)\\n- [ ] Decision recorded with evidence: adopt, adapt, or reject \u2014 with rationale\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **explore attempts/max_attempts counters on sam tasks replacing prose retry rules** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: graph-engineering research comparison\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"e9769943202d7822cdfe76afae9048de8841c18725579d595e26a4de97a0c1d4\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIpD5Q\"}"}